feat: recipes, durability-enchant tag, MIT license

Recipes (user request): every tier is 8 ingots in a ring around the previous tier,
exactly like a furnace; netherite goes through the smithing table.
  cooked_beef -> copper_beef -> iron_beef -> golden_beef -> diamond_beef
  diamond_beef + netherite_upgrade_smithing_template + netherite_ingot -> netherite_beef

This needed a custom Ingredient on all three platforms. All five beefs are the SAME
item, and vanilla matches ingredients by item type alone (1.20.1 does not even look
at NBT; 1.21+ uses SIMPLE_ITEM_CODEC and compares only item+count), so vanilla
cannot express "the middle slot must be the previous tier". The new
orefood:ore_food ingredient matches on the definition id instead:
  { "type": "orefood:ore_food", "definition": "orefood:copper_beef" }

- 1.20.1: AbstractIngredient + IIngredientSerializer via CraftingHelper.register
- 1.21.1 / 26.1.2: ICustomIngredient + IngredientType. 26.1.2 renamed the display
  method to items() returning Stream<Holder<Item>>, and StreamCodec.composite only
  accepts 6 fields, so the now-7-field stream codec is hand-written.

Recipe results carry the definition via nbt (1.20.1) or components (1.21+).
The definition record gained an optional id field for the ingredient to match on.

Durability enchantments (user request): vanilla unbreaking/mending use
supported_items = #minecraft:enchantable/durability and our beef was not in it, so
they could not be applied at all. Added that item tag on 1.21.1/26.1.2; 1.20.1 needs
nothing because EnchantmentCategory.BREAKABLE tests Item#canBeDepleted.

License (user request): All Rights Reserved -> MIT, plus a LICENSE file.

Verified on a dedicated server: 1295 recipes loaded, up from 1290 - exactly our 5 -
with no parsing errors, 5 definitions loaded, and the enchantment tag self-check
unchanged.